  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.TextMeshPro::TMPro.TextMeshProUGUI
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: '1. Turn play is:

    a. Draw a card from the draw deck

    b. Play a
    card

    This may in the form of laying down an Element to the middle of the
    table, or you can play a Formula and take the needed elements from the middle
    to create that formula.

    2. When playing Elements each element has it''s
    own stack of cards. You put them in a pile and can only take the top card from
    that pile as an element. You may not take other elements below that top one.
    For each unique element you lay down on the table, you get 1 point.

    3. Playing
    Formulas: When playing a formula that requires only elements, take the elements
    you need from the middle to create the formula. When playing a formula that requires
    other formulas, take the Formula from yourself and/or other players and use those
    cards to create your new formula. Score the points on the forumla card you just
    created, and if you used other players forumlas too, score 1/2 the points of
    the formula for them as well.

    If you used any formula to make another formula,
    you then take the elements that were on that formula and put them back into play
    in the middle of the table as elements. You do not score points for those elements.

    4.
    Spell Cards:

    You can use them as a spell or element. If you choose spell,
    you do the action:

    a. Eureka Spell: A player can take any one of the top
    cards from the desk of elements for a formula that you may need later. You may
    not take another spell card! Play the spell card and place it at the bottom of
    the element stack that it represents. You immediately play another card from
    your hand. You can play that card as a SPELL, ELEMENT, or FORMULA as described
    in those rules above.

    b.Decomposition Spell: This allows you to destroy
    a Formula that you created earlier in the game. When you destroy that formula,
    you may keep that formula or keep one of the cards that represented that formula
    (except for a spell card). Put all other cards back into the Desk of Elements
    with Spell cards being placed at the bottom of the particular element it represents.

    c.Transformation
    Spell: You take a Formula you created and replace it to the element pile that
    it has on the card to the center. Prior to that replacement you take the top
    card from that element pile and use it as a Formula in front of you. You do not
    score the points for that new formula. Then you take another turn reducing your
    hand by one card. After everyone else goes and your next turn comes back to you
    you draw two cards instead of the one card you normally draw.

    5.Supreme
    Elixir and Supreme Talisman of Magic cards:

    When playing these cards to
    the element desk you can score 0-3 points based on if the elements on these cards
    are already in play or not. Place these as seperate element cards. When someone
    wants to use the elements on these cards to create a formula, they may only choose
    one of the elements, but not 2 or 3.

    6.When you return element cards from
    Formulas that have been stolen or upgraded, and two or more elements are the
    same, you may rearrange the stack for that element to set a particular card on
    top of that element stack. However you must still put all spell cards that are
    in that element stack at the bottom of the stack.

    7. When the deck is empty
    (triggering the near end of game) play continues until all players have played
    all cards in their hand. With no cards remaining, the scores will then be announced
    and a winner will be declared.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
